## Overall Assessment

**Summary:** The paper proposes CODA (Continuous Online Dual-Adaptive Thresholding), a principled online controller that adjusts per-group decision thresholds during deployment to maintain demographic parity under distributional shifts and adversarial stream orderings. It uses a primal-dual optimization framework, initializes from calibrated static thresholds, and adapts online by tracking cumulative fairness constraint violations via a dual variable. The authors prove \(O(\sqrt{T})\) cumulative fairness violation, establish a worst-case asymptotic separation from static thresholding, and demonstrate empirically across five datasets and two model families that CODA achieves higher DPR than static thresholds while maintaining comparable accuracy. A multi-group extension and a substantial governance analysis are also provided.

